#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087401 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b022811-fdd6-56a1-92ee-388e7ea53a4c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the X-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "a66e0264-85ff-5ded-af5d-dc17a9278226" :question/math-academy-id "e-3231" :question/problem "Find the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x + 3)^2 + (y - 2)^2 = 4$." :question/worked-solution "We must have $y = 0$ at the $x$-intercepts.\n\nSo, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 3)^{2} + (y - 2)^{2} &= 4 \\\\\n(x + 3)^{2} + (0 - 2)^{2} &= 4 \\\\\n(x + 3)^{2} + 4 &= 4 \\\\\n(x + 3)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx + 3 &= 0 \\\\\nx &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept occurs at the point $(-3,0)$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/844/Source/Images/7b22a1d5c4a7451aec2ac41c916b9eed.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087402 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9faf6d8-0c06-5bf4-bc41-6d68d3d414c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Circle Does Not Intersect an Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "905861f1-14b5-59f8-980e-6bd962740620" :question/math-academy-id "e-3232" :question/problem "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the circle $(x - 3)^2 + (y - 1)^2 = 4$." :question/worked-solution "We must have $x = 0$ at the $y$-intercepts.\n\nSo, we need to substitute $x = 0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $y$, if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 3)^{2} + (y - 1)^{2} &= 4 \\\\\n(0 - 3)^{2} + (y - 1)^{2} &= 4 \\\\\n9 + (y - 1)^{2} &= 4 \\\\\n(y - 1)^{2} &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n(y-1)^2 \\geq 0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $y$, there is no $y$ such that\n\n$$\n(y-1)^2 = -5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle has no $y$-intercepts, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/844/Source/Images/95fe7135f0f55209cacb7cc2b7b423fa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087403 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6f0c89e-fe19-5aaf-9f85-b5f0ff7a344e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the Y-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "31c41251-e19b-5933-b5f5-6cee31f7f633" :question/math-academy-id "e-3233" :question/problem "Find the $y$-intercepts of the circle $x^2+y^2+2x-2y= 8$, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/844/Source/Images/0ca0fbe99c8a02c276d3fd0a1905e8ae.png)" :question/worked-solution "We must have $x = 0$ at the $y$-intercepts.\n\nSo, we need to substitute $x = 0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + y^{2} + 2x - 2y &= 8 \\\\\n(0)^{2} + y^{2} + 2(0) - 2y &= 8 \\\\\ny^{2} - 2y &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} - 2y + 1 &= 8 + 1 \\\\\n(y - 1)^{2} &= 9 \\\\\ny - 1 &= ± 3 \\\\\ny &= 1 ± 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts occur at $(0,-2)$ and $(0,4)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b022811-fdd6-56a1-92ee-388e7ea53a4c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the X-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcdf2158-3d8a-5577-a89c-c0df108c953b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(10,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267695} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc06385-baa7-55c8-8c76-d5a77b5f8a20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,0)$ and $(12,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267696} {:answer/id #uuid "d028f383-5074-5ba8-a4ea-3fa28fe1ad90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3-\\sqrt{6},0)$ and $(3+\\sqrt{6},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267697} {:answer/id #uuid "c68c7ff9-ef71-5444-8b2e-bd8e379d5ea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3-\\sqrt{5},0)$ and $(3+\\sqrt{5},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267698} {:answer/id #uuid "546f2032-21f5-59b2-a85b-59b5f7a0dce3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3-\\sqrt{7},0)$ and $(3+\\sqrt{7},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "546f2032-21f5-59b2-a85b-59b5f7a0dce3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3-\\sqrt{7},0)$ and $(3+\\sqrt{7},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267699} :answer-field/id #uuid "45b74600-dd65-5daf-a0bb-21f135aef473" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267694}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "acf4151c-fa50-5ea8-9858-28080dd21e43" :question/math-academy-id "q-15471" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959190/assets/27e00721ccddb4982dac95216cdd990af311e27e5300154b7133b36fca42f3e1.png)\n\nFind the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=8,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(0-1)^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+1 & =8 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =7 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{7} \\\\ x & =3\\pm \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts occur at the points $(3-\\sqrt{7},0)$ and $(3+\\sqrt{7},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b022811-fdd6-56a1-92ee-388e7ea53a4c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the X-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c110050-6686-51ea-b186-39ebaa097fc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-9,0)$ and $(1.5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267702} {:answer/id #uuid "53a5b66d-7124-5fb4-89e2-22105b1af00b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-8,0)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267703} {:answer/id #uuid "0b27c99d-2398-5a19-97c5-cd11f2ed14fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-9,0)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267704} {:answer/id #uuid "a563ed93-7ce7-57eb-a026-d74c058e5bb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-10,0)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267705} {:answer/id #uuid "a70833ad-924a-544c-94e9-73e797db0e89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-9,0)$ and $(0.8,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b27c99d-2398-5a19-97c5-cd11f2ed14fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-9,0)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267704} :answer-field/id #uuid "12d6d6df-5bb6-5bc5-8e62-6a1b35ee1cf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267701}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "128b9cef-763e-5aac-a6f0-9eae06355cd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-15681" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959190/assets/13b9598c77cd522d0d3aeee4f5abd2390822431aa49ed787b73a110113ab821c.png)\n\nFind the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=25,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =25 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+{0}^{2} & =25 \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =25 \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{25} \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm 5 \\\\ x & =-4\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts occur at the points $(-9,0)$ and $(1,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267707 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b022811-fdd6-56a1-92ee-388e7ea53a4c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the X-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb9dad29-b3f6-5ffd-b193-ffc7c83f5a12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-5,0)$ and $(8,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267709} {:answer/id #uuid "d6982368-5a66-5f9d-9a66-f5fdb0984bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-5.5,0)$ and $(7.5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267710} {:answer/id #uuid "27bad4bf-ea6b-5517-8b75-4bf1337a756d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-6,0)$ and $(8,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267711} {:answer/id #uuid "d7258ddb-0a7e-558c-9131-5108b75c2a7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-5,0)$ and $(7,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267712} {:answer/id #uuid "5e105f04-51ef-5fab-90ac-f2f2958c85cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-6,0)$ and $(7,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7258ddb-0a7e-558c-9131-5108b75c2a7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-5,0)$ and $(7,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267712} :answer-field/id #uuid "d38519d4-cc52-5902-9cf3-70f4e5db357c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267708}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d50f11f-d6f2-5605-88f1-8c45f7404cb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-15684" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959190/assets/c6d356b443128da569a6b60065769cdb56e2e0059db7b146a3c4035d18a32228.png)\n\nFind the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x-1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=45,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =45 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(0-3)^{2} & =45 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+9 & =45 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =36 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{36} \\\\ x & =1\\pm 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts occur at the points $(-5,0)$ and $(7,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6f0c89e-fe19-5aaf-9f85-b5f0ff7a344e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a584fb00-fe9c-557f-9554-d4a8d1b5a475" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2-\\sqrt{7})$ and $(0,2+\\sqrt{7})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267716} {:answer/id #uuid "e610e161-e174-5b3d-9956-f1802731611c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2.5)$ and $(0,4.5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267717} {:answer/id #uuid "db138d67-d129-570e-a2e9-332c0ec96972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1-\\sqrt{7})$ and $(0,1+\\sqrt{7})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267718} {:answer/id #uuid "43172982-8a98-5ebb-a625-b0875158f0e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1-\\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,1+\\sqrt{5})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267719} {:answer/id #uuid "99ed0af9-5c69-5166-ba34-eb717bc7b6e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2)$ and $(0,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db138d67-d129-570e-a2e9-332c0ec96972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1-\\sqrt{7})$ and $(0,1+\\sqrt{7})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267718} :answer-field/id #uuid "0effc939-8610-5186-9232-7341ad20fa9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267715}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29d9da5c-80e6-50a1-9b76-879967d6afda" :question/math-academy-id "q-15691" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959190/assets/4466cdfbce1903942ae6aa857cec8e831f0e7f819f2e6771086919918d8f4110.png)\n\nFind the $y$-intercepts of the circle $(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=16,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We must have $x=0$ at the $y$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (0-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ 9+(y-1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =7 \\\\ y-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{7} \\\\ y & =1\\pm \\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts occur at the points $(0,1-\\sqrt{7})$ and $(0,1+\\sqrt{7}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6f0c89e-fe19-5aaf-9f85-b5f0ff7a344e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "365c2cc9-c09c-5186-a5b2-cbc1b37e4c50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,4-\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,4+\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267723} {:answer/id #uuid "b7767f20-469a-5b49-9722-5ca0d4953b10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,6-\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,6+\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267724} {:answer/id #uuid "2d992dcf-09b9-53f5-8bca-d6c180748bcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,5-\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,5+\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267725} {:answer/id #uuid "4058f0e9-0f05-56bc-9830-4c394c1368e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,5-\\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,5+\\sqrt{5})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267726} {:answer/id #uuid "fa03cb03-a289-5f78-ab13-b8ebd0c4b8c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,4-\\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,4+\\sqrt{5})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa03cb03-a289-5f78-ab13-b8ebd0c4b8c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,4-\\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,4+\\sqrt{5})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267727} :answer-field/id #uuid "12d7b9fc-d7a3-5bd7-a0a9-50ebbbfef39b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267722}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b39c00d5-06a8-557a-8fa0-abcc975f62c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-15692" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959190/assets/185393f07b920bf9b72799f1c17c4245017b500ff1ca92a0104fe95ce621bde4.png)\n\nFind the $y$-intercepts of the circle $(x-2)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=9,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We must have $x=0$ at the $y$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (0-2)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ 4+(y-4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =9-4 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =5 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5} \\\\ y & =4\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts occur at the points $(0,4-\\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,4+\\sqrt{5}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9faf6d8-0c06-5bf4-bc41-6d68d3d414c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Circle Does Not Intersect an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af74ac86-06fd-5d63-a4e4-45c8bfb8357d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267730} {:answer/id #uuid "1349e6f0-2289-55e4-93ec-b2ad8f16b6f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-1)$ and $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267731} {:answer/id #uuid "7a540a32-1f0e-5244-9c2f-533e28055674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle has no $y$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267732} {:answer/id #uuid "4a515f53-0ed3-5dca-8ffe-a6fda60873fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267733} {:answer/id #uuid "61a3cfe9-fbfb-501a-9c5c-6eac1308683f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-7)$ and $(0,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a540a32-1f0e-5244-9c2f-533e28055674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle has no $y$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267732} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca2a37aa-af08-58f9-aa50-893dda7203bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267729}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26d19767-6cb6-5671-822b-38e5862a3bbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-160272" :question/problem "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the circle $(x-4)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=9.$" :question/worked-solution "When the circle intercepts the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$\n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $y$, if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (0-4)^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ 16+(y+4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (y+4)^{2} & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(y+4)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $y,$ there is no $y$ such that $(y+4)^{2}=-7.$ Therefore, the circle does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959190/assets/2442ca38770f2f82e2e9c536632e8474adb75e04504a4273987989bb3d81b282.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9faf6d8-0c06-5bf4-bc41-6d68d3d414c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Circle Does Not Intersect an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f80d2f64-e788-5621-b7da-23c2453a1f04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267737} {:answer/id #uuid "1e0ec51b-3660-51a8-8a40-4363ad19d3e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-\\frac{1}{4})$ and $(0,\\frac{3}{4})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267738} {:answer/id #uuid "0cac8261-80b4-5649-bf1c-1e69a56d7d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267739} {:answer/id #uuid "68c8d89a-acbc-5908-ac1e-e86036864292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle has no $y$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267740} {:answer/id #uuid "fef2a79c-2a6d-5510-964f-0c0548484a19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68c8d89a-acbc-5908-ac1e-e86036864292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle has no $y$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267740} :answer-field/id #uuid "7067af39-0c6e-514e-aa79-74762099ea15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267736}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8baf38e5-4cac-5611-8d4f-60ea7baafdea" :question/math-academy-id "q-160275" :question/problem "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the circle $(x+1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{3}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "When the circle intercepts the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$\n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $y$, if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ (0+1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1+(y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(y+2)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $y,$ there is no $y$ such that $(y+2)^{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, the circle does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959190/assets/86f198b30096cf3106186ee2595b61c21be000e8e6223c5b42ee453ba1b9fe4a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9faf6d8-0c06-5bf4-bc41-6d68d3d414c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Circle Does Not Intersect an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "617357b4-289b-538c-b217-0c0ded8e0779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-5,0)$ and $(12,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267744} {:answer/id #uuid "8d97a31f-4600-5899-8782-618d32a0bde2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267745} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f25041-4a4a-51d2-990a-8422d912e219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle has no $x$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267746} {:answer/id #uuid "431c93d4-6b45-5cc5-9206-0dd6e6594267" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-6,0)$ and $(-2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267747} {:answer/id #uuid "296a331e-81c0-521f-b671-4e6e84d7ccc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2f25041-4a4a-51d2-990a-8422d912e219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle has no $x$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267746} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a2d023b-07b4-58b0-a1cf-3b4676decdb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267743}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75748ec5-c482-5439-91c2-00c020d6415b" :question/math-academy-id "q-160286" :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+8x-6y+21=0$" :question/worked-solution "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts.  \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+8x-6y+21 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{0}^{2}+8x-6\\cdot 0+21 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x+21 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+8x+16+21 & =0+16 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+21 & =16 \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x+4)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x,$ there is no $x$ such that $(x+4)^{2}=-5.$ Therefore, circle has no $x$-intercepts, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959190/assets/53b8e33665ee3ba0b36d04c9d82715b18505c501db535ecf4a40c48c81814584.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267749 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b022811-fdd6-56a1-92ee-388e7ea53a4c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the X-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a3825ca-450c-5fec-9292-9c6eeb9f2346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,0)$ and $(4,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267751} {:answer/id #uuid "7487f0e9-dc1e-550a-a3af-691fe0e777c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,0)$ and $(5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267752} {:answer/id #uuid "7d1c6651-2b34-51ae-9785-cded765103ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,0)$ and $(4,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267753} {:answer/id #uuid "72d8bfd4-cfc9-5e86-a257-cf5065fcc175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,0)$ and $(5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267754} {:answer/id #uuid "482186d3-3759-5756-9324-f4ce5925a332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4,0)$ and $(6,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7487f0e9-dc1e-550a-a3af-691fe0e777c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,0)$ and $(5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267752} :answer-field/id #uuid "165a59ce-50e6-5a0d-bad8-003375710839" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267750}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1aef1d02-462f-5423-aa4a-1388b5adbed6" :question/math-academy-id "q-54228" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959190/assets/3f597a8596601b6e53e93e76ba3082d87159ff03b52c2a5a2b3eba5cbf3332ec.png)\n\nFind the $x$-intercepts of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x+6y+15=0,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x+6y+15 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-8x+6\\cdot 0+15 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+15 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x+16+15 & =0+16 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+15 & =16 \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =1 \\\\ x-4 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =4\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts occur at the points $(3,0)$ and $(5,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267756 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6f0c89e-fe19-5aaf-9f85-b5f0ff7a344e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "781d0282-5d88-5f76-925f-eaaf0598d46e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2\\sqrt{2})$ and $(0,2\\sqrt{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267758} {:answer/id #uuid "2f7fdbd6-2e40-5aa7-a80b-7ef24ee8fed3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-8)$ and $(0,8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267759} {:answer/id #uuid "f55a9efb-ade0-54c8-97a8-a5a2a84e90e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267760} {:answer/id #uuid "2c457160-b00f-54f1-ade5-bbdd8d4ed1c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-64)$ and $(0,64)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267761} {:answer/id #uuid "070db0b8-ee36-59b2-9d7f-3aa08f4b409a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-4)$ and $(0,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f7fdbd6-2e40-5aa7-a80b-7ef24ee8fed3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-8)$ and $(0,8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267759} :answer-field/id #uuid "220d46e7-78a0-5757-b155-742f09840087" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267757}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2b39999-a8e4-57a7-ab96-4715ec5c76ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-7541" :question/problem "Find the $y$-intercepts of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=64.$" :question/worked-solution "We must have $x=0$ at the $y$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =64 \\\\ {0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =64 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =64 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{64} \\\\ y & =\\pm 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y$-intercepts occur at the points $(0,-8)$ and $(0,8),$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959190/assets/90fc285151a5f549992e3c40ba6cff89532398808d6194fdf7922108b73646ef.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9faf6d8-0c06-5bf4-bc41-6d68d3d414c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Circle Does Not Intersect an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c77d996-98a0-53e9-8d8e-30d0603083f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle has no $x$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267765} {:answer/id #uuid "ac011ff3-3ebb-5e07-bc82-18c3da00bc3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267766} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2d8884-19b3-5616-a2b6-3540fb01f857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1-\\sqrt{11},0)$ and $(1+\\sqrt{11},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267767} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff30ae4-14c3-5268-a6fa-8fc0b9d3f34e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,0)$ and $(5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267768} {:answer/id #uuid "d51b8305-968a-5af3-9adc-481a1ac8010c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{11},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c77d996-98a0-53e9-8d8e-30d0603083f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle has no $x$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267765} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d45a237-1e48-5551-a790-007c77780796" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267764}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f43a31ca-f582-5ca4-a303-c0af1e9c11d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-79044" :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x+6y+12=0$" :question/worked-solution "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts.  \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x+6y+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2x+6\\cdot 0+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+1+12 & =0+1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+12 & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x-1)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x,$ there is no $x$ such that $(x-1)^{2}=-11.$ Therefore, circle has no $x$-intercepts, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959190/assets/ea1410ea792ac5fb09316bc6d417c2ba46f84c050491b1e351f360eac50deb6c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267770 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b022811-fdd6-56a1-92ee-388e7ea53a4c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the X-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "756f46d3-5b70-5001-bccc-09df1ee2b662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(5-\\sqrt{6},0)$ and $(5+\\sqrt{3},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267772} {:answer/id #uuid "9eddda51-7da5-559b-98c9-47dbe5c56854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1.5,0)$ and $(6.5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267773} {:answer/id #uuid "bc0983d8-128f-523d-bfd9-3455323a0dbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4-\\sqrt{5},0)$ and $(4+\\sqrt{5},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267774} {:answer/id #uuid "55280699-f27d-5e7f-b8cb-82f2b53f6041" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4-\\sqrt{6},0)$ and $(4+\\sqrt{6},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267775} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8a5b6c-828e-53e3-9366-d9212d783028" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(5-\\sqrt{6},0)$ and $(4+\\sqrt{5},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55280699-f27d-5e7f-b8cb-82f2b53f6041" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4-\\sqrt{6},0)$ and $(4+\\sqrt{6},0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267775} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1ba8ed2-95db-56f3-a55d-b7f0d8bdec6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267771}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff9bf7eb-98f6-5135-b8bc-3f9227c2a773" :question/math-academy-id "q-8789" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959190/assets/37a9d6fca5d60e8f614730a121e1f5da23011e6c7a8ea80840a16b81e376638b.png)\n\nFind the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x-4)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=10,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(0+2)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+4 & =10 \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =6 \\\\ x-4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{6} \\\\ x & =4\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts occur at the points $(4-\\sqrt{6},0)$ and $(4+\\sqrt{6},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267777 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6f0c89e-fe19-5aaf-9f85-b5f0ff7a344e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b29add4-9643-5235-9826-dc3fc1865863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267779} {:answer/id #uuid "346a216a-762a-5172-ba82-1bc90c0b8aa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267780} {:answer/id #uuid "7abc7073-007f-5014-b1e6-9f429626ca83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267781} {:answer/id #uuid "862590b2-6239-5f26-9af2-bae9d320d21c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267782} {:answer/id #uuid "363723c5-f847-5c72-ab88-ef9b79c6a5b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b29add4-9643-5235-9826-dc3fc1865863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267779} :answer-field/id #uuid "a01b237d-38aa-5f23-95fe-7b9c76f4309f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267778}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5cbb3827-affd-5557-9bcc-dc99637fb97d" :question/math-academy-id "q-8794" :question/problem "Calculate the $y$-intercept of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x-2y+1=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We must have $x=0$ at the $y$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x-2y+1 & =0 \\\\ {0}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4\\cdot 0-2y+1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+1 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-1 & =0 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y$-intercept occurs at the point $(0,1),$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959190/assets/87766af5840b6b3729fa200d383e18c5f7926d9000633f99b481e8b4a6922732.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6f0c89e-fe19-5aaf-9f85-b5f0ff7a344e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa5c11f3-5c54-54fc-a861-9f928a7b8c32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-3)$ and $(0,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267786} {:answer/id #uuid "74c181bd-f376-5d04-b151-565d309e1a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-4)$ and $(0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267787} {:answer/id #uuid "b9495a7b-92d3-5b0a-908a-9af5fac3f477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-5)$ and $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267788} {:answer/id #uuid "284bf67d-5b73-5274-bf59-f85fd02814f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-1)$ and $(0,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267789} {:answer/id #uuid "cc006d2f-e00d-583c-b45b-31f54a67ed77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2)$ and $(0,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9495a7b-92d3-5b0a-908a-9af5fac3f477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-5)$ and $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267788} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a793a8a-a012-502b-8df7-acc553aa1e73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267785}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f32d89de-5253-5122-b1ef-0e2ab36feec6" :question/math-academy-id "q-8795" :question/problem "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-x+4y=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We must have $x=0$ at the $y$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-x+4y & =5 \\\\ {0}^{2}+{y}^{2}-0+4y & =5 \\\\ {y}^{2}+4y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+4y+4 & =5+4 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =-2\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y$-intercepts occur at the points $(0,-5)$ and $(0,1),$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959190/assets/1e4f7c975282aee647b2fa161ba34abac5c19e6bc3fc68f812533a0eb527834a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088385 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9faf6d8-0c06-5bf4-bc41-6d68d3d414c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Circle Does Not Intersect an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f34793f4-d1bc-55ef-ae53-5c1f899e1dca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle has no $x$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087241} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf2c342-a2a1-504b-9f09-bce2765f6388" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4, 0)$ and $(-2, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087242} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb476fe-3ff9-5dd8-a59a-816f32f1cc85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087243} {:answer/id #uuid "4e729635-4669-5cc0-a772-4b13036834c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087244} {:answer/id #uuid "526883e8-3397-542b-8212-e2dc7e759528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4, 0)$ and $(2, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f34793f4-d1bc-55ef-ae53-5c1f899e1dca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle has no $x$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087241} :answer-field/id #uuid "b85c01aa-1d3f-537a-9d1a-63a72a7d7810" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087240}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb231c9b-8ce3-5939-ba7a-76e32364f988" :question/math-academy-id "q-8801" :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x + 3)^{2} + (y - 2)^{2} = 3$" :question/worked-solution "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts.  \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(0-2)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+4 & =3 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x+3)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x,$ there is no $x$ such that $(x+3)^{2}=-1.$ Therefore, circle has no $x$-intercepts, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959190/assets/cb8a943a6f6cfd49e47c193f5379e06fd166cd17087c890233b42a7e1e7d2b89.png)"}]}
